  "text": "㠓第一批: 🌲 halkın\n\n\tmemset(삲, CStringT(\"[%02d] \").upInt(תכנית).pcString(), 40); \nmemset(.SetKeyName, CStringT(\"Receiving package number [%02d] \").upInt(תכנית).pcString(), 40);\n\tmemset( intimacam, CStringT(\"[%02d] \").upInt(תכנית).pcString(), 40);\n\tmemset(专班Endem, CStringT(\"[%02d] \").upInt(תכנית).pcString(), 40);\n\tmemset(专班버림, CStringT(\"[🀄100] \").upInt(תכנית).pcString(), 40); To find the least possible total number of meals catering services need to prepare, we first need to understand the meal requirements and ratios across all three groups: athletes, coaches, and officials. We aim to distribute the same variety of food items (chicken sandwiches, vegetarian sandwiches, and salads) in equal proportions to each group, while minimizing the total number of meals.\n\nLet's define the scenario:\n- Athletes receive large boxes with:\n  - 3 chicken sandwiches per box\n  - 2 vegetarian sandwiches per box\n  - 3 salads per box\n- Coaches receive smaller boxes with:\n  - 2 chicken sandwiches per box\n  - 1 vegetarian sandwich per box\n  - 2 salads per box\n- Officials receive individual meal packages with:\n  - 1 chicken sandwich per package\n  - 1 vegetarian sandwich per package\n  - 1 salad per package\n\nTo satisfy the conditions, we need to ensure that the total number of each type of meal is divisible by the number of groups. This ensures that each group gets an equal ratio of the same variety of food items.\n\nLet's denote the number of large boxes for athletes as \\(A\\), the number of smaller boxes for coaches as \\(C\\), and the number of packages for officials as \\(P\\). We want to minimize \\(A + C + P\\) while ensuring that the number of each type of meal is divisible by the number of groups in each case.\n\nFor athletes:\n- Total chicken sandwiches: \\(3A\\)\n- Total vegetarian sandwiches: \\(2A\\)\n- Total salads: \\(3A\\)\n\nFor coaches:\n- Total chicken sandwiches: \\(2C\\)\n- Total vegetarian sandwiches: \\(C\\)\n- Total salads: \\(2C\\)\n\nFor officials:\n- Total chicken sandwiches: \\(P\\)\n- Total vegetarian sandwiches: \\(P\\)\n- Total salads: \\(P\\)\n\nTo minimize the total number of meals, we can start by setting \\(A\\), \\(C\\), and \\(P\\) to the smallest values that make the total count of each type of meal divisible by 3 (for athletes) and 2 (for coaches). We'll then adjust these values to meet the minimum requirement for officials.\n\n1. For athletes, we need \\(3A\\) (chicken), \\(2A\\) (vegetarian), and \\(3A\\) (salads) to be divisible by 3, so the smallest value for \\(A\\) is 1. This satisfies everything, giving us 3 chicken, 2 vegetarian, and 3 salad per box.\n\n2. For coaches, we need \\(2C\\) (chicken), \\(C\\) (vegetarian), and \\(2C\\) (salads) to be divisible by 2, so the smallest value for \\(C\\) is 1. This gives us 2 chicken, 1 vegetarian, and 2 salad per box, which meets the requirements as 2 divides into 2 evenly.\n\n3. For officials, we need \\(P\\) (chicken), \\(P\\) (vegetarian), and \\(P\\) (salad) to be divisible by 3 and 2. Since it's a minimum requirement, we need at least \\(3 \\times 2 = 6\\) in total, so the smallest \\(P\\) is 6.\n\nThus, the least possible total number of meals is:\n- Athletes: \\(3^3 = 27\\) (all large boxes with 3 chicken, 2 vegetarian, 3 salad each)\n- Coaches: \\(2^1 = 2\\) (all smaller boxes with 2 chicken, 1 vegetarian, 2 salad each)\n- Officials: \\(6\\) (all meals for officials, with 100% of each item)\n\nTotal meals = \\(27 + 2 + 6 = 35\\)\n\nSo, the boxed final answer is 35.",
